        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Before You Buy: Which Kind of Wet Wall Do You Have?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a plastic sheet, not a shopping cart. Cut a square of clear polyethylene about 18 inches on each side, tape all four edges tight to the bare wall, and leave it for a day or two. That is the method behind ASTM D4263, the standard moisture check that coating manufacturers reference. Where the beads form tells you which problem you are actually trying to solve.
          </p>

          <p>
            Droplets on the room side of the plastic mean warm, humid basement air is condensing on a cool wall. No sealer fixes that, because the water is not coming through the concrete. A <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a>, better air circulation and insulation will. Droplets trapped behind the plastic, against the wall, mean moisture is wicking through the masonry from the soil. That is the case a wall sealer is built for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look for patterns. A dark band along the bottom foot of the wall, white crusty salt deposits, or a crack that weeps after heavy rain all point to water in the soil outside. A steady trickle or a jet of water through a tie hole or crack is a different scale of problem: that is active water under pressure, and it needs plugging and drainage before any coating can hold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down three things before you shop: the wall material (poured concrete or hollow block), whether the wall is bare or has old paint, and the worst behavior you have seen, from damp to seeping to running. Those three answers narrow the seven picks above to one or two in about a minute, and they keep you from buying a coating for a job that needs a shovel.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more clue is timing. Condensation peaks in humid summer weather, often on the coolest stretch of wall, and fades when the furnace runs. Seepage tracks rainfall and snowmelt, shows up a day or two after a storm and lingers at the base of the wall. Keep a simple log for a few weeks of what the wall looks like after rain versus after a muggy, dry spell, and the pattern usually becomes obvious.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Condensation is an air problem. Seepage is a wall problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the plastic-sheet check shows moisture on the room side, spend your money on dehumidification first. Only moisture that collects behind the sheet is something a wall sealer can address.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Four Sealer Chemistries and the Job Each One Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Cement-and-latex masonry waterproofers</strong> such as DRYLOK are thick coatings loaded with fine solids. Brushed hard into the surface, they fill the pores of block and concrete and leave a film that resists water pushing from behind. They change the look of the wall, since they go on like paint, and they need bare, clean masonry to bond. For an interior block wall that seeps, this is the default choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating sealers</strong>, including silicate-based products like RadonSeal and silane-siloxane water repellents, soak into the concrete and react below the surface. They leave no film, so there is nothing to peel, and the wall looks the same afterward. Silicates densify concrete by filling capillaries; silane-siloxanes make the pore walls repel water. Both need an unpainted surface to soak into.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Elastomeric liquid rubber coatings</strong> such as Liquid Rubber and Ames Block and Wall cure into a stretchy membrane. Their selling point is flexibility: they can bridge hairline cracks that open and close with the seasons. They are at their best on the exterior of a foundation, or on interior walls that see dampness rather than real pressure, because a rubber film pushed off the wall from behind will bubble.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Crystalline waterproofing</strong>, sold under names like Xypex, is a cement slurry with reactive chemicals that grow crystals inside the concrete when water is present. It is a professional-grade system used on tanks and below-grade structures. It works on poured concrete, needs a saturated surface and careful mixing, and is far less forgiving of shortcuts than a can of masonry paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick way to choose among them: film coatings are for walls you will look at and want dry; penetrants are for walls you want to keep as bare concrete; elastomerics are for walls that crack and move; crystalline slurries are for poured walls where you want a permanent, professional treatment. Most homeowners with block foundations end up with a film coating, and most with sound poured walls do well with a penetrant.
          </p>

