        <div id="pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Hydrostatic Pressure Ratings, Explained in Plain Numbers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water in saturated soil pushes on a foundation with a force that grows with depth. Each foot of water above a point adds roughly 0.43 psi. A wall with the water table 8 feet up sees about 3.5 psi at the base. That is why a coating rated for 10 or 15 psi sounds like overkill, but those figures come from a lab test on a sample, not from your wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The label rating tells you which coatings were built to stay bonded with water pushing from behind them. DRYLOK Original lists 10 psi; DRYLOK Extreme lists 15 psi. Elastomeric interior coatings usually carry no such rating, and penetrating sealers do not have one because there is no film to push off. Treat the number as a tiebreaker between film coatings, not a promise.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the rating does not cover matters more. A coating cannot stop water from entering along the seam between slab and wall (the cove joint), it cannot hold back water pouring through a crack, and it does nothing for water rising through the slab. If you see water in those places, the wall coating is a secondary measure and drainage is the primary one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What the Labels Say</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DRYLOK Extreme rating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DRYLOK Original rating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0.43 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added per foot of water depth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cure before coating new concrete</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A practical way to read all this: if your wall is damp or seeps slowly after storms, any well-applied masonry waterproofer is strong enough. If water runs, the rating is beside the point. Fix grading, gutters and cracks first, then coat. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">waterproofing a basement from the outside in</a> covers the order in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Sealer a Basement Actually Takes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the wall area you will coat, not the floor. Multiply the perimeter of the basement by the height of the bare wall, then subtract large openings. A 30-by-40-foot basement has 140 feet of perimeter; at 7.5 feet of exposed wall, that is about 1,050 square feet. Partial-height walls in a walkout or a basement with a finished section shrink that number quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Masonry waterproofers spread far less than wall paint because the first coat disappears into the pores. DRYLOK lists roughly 75 to 100 square feet per gallon for the first coat on smooth concrete, and less on rough or split-face block. The second coat goes further. For the 1,050-square-foot example, plan on 11 to 14 gallons for coat one and 8 to 10 for coat two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers depend on how thirsty the concrete is. Dense poured walls may take 150 to 200 square feet per gallon, while open-textured block can drink twice as much. The maker of each product lists a range, and the honest move is to buy for the low end of it and return unopened containers. Elastomeric coatings are applied thicker and typically land around 50 to 75 square feet per gallon per coat.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
