          <p>
            Buy all the coating from the same batch if you can. A half-finished wall waiting three days for another gallon will have a lap line where the new coat meets the old, and with film coatings a cold joint is a weak spot. Have a brush, a roller with a 3/4-inch nap and a pail of clean water ready before you open the first can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Prep That Decides Whether It Holds
          </h2>

          <p>
            A masonry coating bonds to masonry, not to dust, paint, salt or mold. Scrape loose paint, then wire-brush every square foot. If the wall has any existing paint that is still well bonded, most masonry waterproofers will not perform as rated over it; the label usually calls for bare surfaces. Penetrating sealers are even stricter, because they cannot soak through a film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Efflorescence, the white powder or crust on the wall, is mineral salt left behind by evaporating water. Brush it off dry, then wash with a masonry cleaner or a mild acid etch made for concrete, and rinse thoroughly. Painting over efflorescence is the single most common reason a basement coating flakes off within a year: the salts keep growing under the film and push it away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair cracks and holes before coating. Hairline cracks can be filled with the coating itself, worked in with the brush. Anything wider than about 1/8 inch needs a masonry patch or a polyurethane crack sealant. An actively leaking hole gets hydraulic cement, which expands as it sets and can stop a trickle in minutes. Let patches cure as directed before coating over them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, kill any mold with a cleaner labeled for the purpose, let the wall dry thoroughly, and check the forecast. Most coatings want a surface and air temperature above 50 degrees Fahrenheit through the cure. Running a dehumidifier for a few days before and after application shortens dry times and keeps the basement air from fogging the fresh film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying a Masonry Waterproofer the Right Way
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stir, do not shake. The solids in a masonry waterproofer settle hard, and a can that has not been mixed to the bottom lays down a thin, watery first coat that never reaches its rated performance. Stir thoroughly and keep stirring every 15 minutes while you work. Box multiple gallons together in a five-gallon pail to keep the color and consistency even.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first coat goes on with a stiff nylon masonry brush, not a roller. The goal is to force the coating into every pinhole and mortar joint. Work in small sections, scrub the coating in, then lay it off in one direction. When you can see no pinholes in the finished area, move on. It is slow, and it is where the performance comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the first coat dry for the time on the label, usually a few hours, then apply the second coat. A roller with a thick nap is acceptable here on smooth poured concrete; on block, keep brushing. Inspect the wall with a work light held flat against the surface. Any pinhole that glows means water can get through it, so dab it closed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilate during the job, and treat solvent-based products with extra care in a basement: open windows, run a fan blowing outward and keep pilot lights and furnaces in mind if the label warns about flammable vapors. Water-based products are easier to live with, but they still release vapor while curing. Give the coating at least the full recommended cure before stacking boxes or framing against the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mind the edges. Cut in around pipes, electrical boxes and the sill plate with a smaller brush, and carry the coating a few inches onto the slab at the base of the wall so the cove joint is covered rather than skipped. Mask the wood sill and any framing you want to keep clean; masonry waterproofer is hard to remove from wood once it sets, and it is not designed to protect lumber anyway.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">PINHOLE CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One pass is never enough on block.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Hollow concrete block has thousands of tiny voids. Plan on two full coats with a brush on the first pass, and check the finished wall with a raking light before you put the tools away.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="inside-outside" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Interior Sealing vs. Exterior Waterproofing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Interior coatings work on what is called the negative side: water reaches the wall from the soil and pushes toward the coating. Exterior membranes work on the positive side, where the water arrives first. Positive-side waterproofing is better engineering, because pressure presses the membrane into the wall instead of trying to peel it off. It is also far more expensive, because it means digging.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, the sensible sequence is to fix the outside cheaply and seal the inside carefully. Extend downspouts several feet from the foundation, regrade so soil falls away from the house (residential building code calls for a drop of 6 inches over the first 10 feet where possible), and keep gutters clear. Those three steps reduce the water a wall sees and make any interior coating far more likely to work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exterior excavation makes sense when the soil around the house stays saturated, when the wall is bowing or cracking structurally, or when you are already digging for another reason. At that point a professional applies a membrane, adds drainage board and sets a footing drain. If you are comparing that route with an interior drain and pump, our ranking of <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/">basement waterproofing systems</a> lays out both.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle option for block walls that are damp but not leaking: a penetrating silane-siloxane sealer applied inside, which reduces wicking without a film that could blister. If you are planning to paint afterward, read our guide to the <a href="/best-paint-for-cinder-block/">best paint for cinder block</a> first, because many penetrating sealers block paint adhesion.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are finishing the basement later, think about the wall coating as one layer in a system. Building science guidance for below-grade walls generally favors rigid foam board against the masonry rather than fiberglass batts and poly sheeting, because foam keeps the wall surface warm and tolerates some moisture. A sealed, dry wall behind that foam is a better base than an untreated one, but the coating is not a vapor barrier for a finished room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Sealing a Basement Wall Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The products are the cheap part. At the time of writing, a gallon of a premium masonry waterproofer runs roughly $35 to $60, a five-gallon pail roughly $150 to $250, and a gallon of penetrating sealer roughly $40 to $90 depending on concentration. For a typical 1,000-square-foot basement, expect to spend somewhere around $300 to $900 on coating and another $50 to $150 on brushes, cleaner and patch material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring the same job out usually costs $3 to $7 per square foot for a cleaned, patched, two-coat interior application, according to typical contractor pricing. That puts a full basement around $3,000 to $7,000. The labor is mostly scrubbing and brushing; anyone comfortable with paint and patience can do it in two or three weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that to the systems a sealer is often sold against. An interior perimeter drain with a sump pump commonly runs $5,000 to $15,000; exterior excavation and membrane work can reach $15,000 to $30,000 or more on a full foundation. A sealer is not a cheaper version of those systems; it is a different, smaller tool. Buy it for dampness and minor seepage and it is good value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Factor in time as well as money. A DIY coating job on a full basement typically means a day of clearing and scrubbing, a day of patching and drying, and two separate days of brushing, spread over a week or more once cure times are counted. Penetrating sealers are quicker to apply, often a single afternoon with a pump sprayer, but the wall still has to be stripped and cleaned first, and that step takes the same effort either way.
          </p>

