            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How the Main Brands Compare on Paper
          </h2>

          <p>
            DRYLOK, made by United Gilsonite Laboratories, dominates the hardware store shelf, and for good reason: it has two clearly differentiated formulas, published pressure ratings, and a data sheet that spells out surface prep in plain language. Its weakness is that it only works on bare masonry, which rules it out for many older basements without stripping first.
          </p>

          <p>
            RadonSeal and similar silicate penetrants are sold mostly online, often directly by the maker, and come with detailed instructions about testing whether concrete will absorb the product. Their appeal is invisibility and permanence, since the reaction happens inside the wall. Their limitation is that hollow block, with its thin shells and open cores, gives them less material to react with than a solid poured wall does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Liquid rubber brands, including Liquid Rubber and Ames, appeal to owners who have cracks that keep reopening. Read their labels closely for the recommended dry film thickness and the number of coats it takes to reach it; a single thin coat of an elastomeric product gives you very little of the flexibility you are paying for. Xypex sits apart as a commercial product with a distributor network and technical support aimed at contractors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, download the technical data sheet before you open the can. It lists the surface temperature range, the recoat window, the minimum age of new concrete, and whether the product can go over existing coatings. Those four details account for most of the coating failures owners describe online, and every one of them is printed in the data sheet before the first brushstroke.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. When a Wall Sealer Is the Wrong Purchase
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do not seal a wall that is moving. Horizontal cracks in block, stair-step cracks through mortar joints, or a wall that bows inward are signs of soil pressure on the structure. A coating will hide the evidence for a season and the movement will continue. Get a structural engineer to look at it; repairs such as wall anchors or bracing come first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not seal a wall to fix a musty smell when the plastic-sheet check showed condensation. The smell comes from humidity and organic material, and the fix is controlling air moisture. Our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> covers that diagnosis and the order of fixes in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use a basement wall sealer as a radon fix. Some penetrating sealers are marketed with radon in the name, and sealing cracks and slabs can reduce soil gas entry, but EPA guidance treats sealing as a supplement to active mitigation, not a substitute. Test the air; if results are at or above 4 pCi/L, call a certified mitigation contractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            And do not coat a wall that floods. If the basement takes in standing water after storms, the water is coming in at the floor joint or through the slab, where a wall coating cannot reach. That basement needs drainage and a sump. Coat the walls afterward if they still feel damp, and you will get far longer life out of the product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over white salt deposits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Efflorescence keeps growing under a fresh film and pushes it off the wall, usually within the first year. Brush it away dry, wash with a masonry cleaner or concrete etch, rinse well and let the wall dry fully before the first coat goes on. If the salt comes back quickly, water is still moving through the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling the first coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roller skims across the pores of block instead of filling them, which leaves thousands of pinholes. The first coat of a masonry waterproofer needs a stiff nylon brush worked into the surface. Save the roller for the second coat on smooth poured concrete, and check for pinholes with a raking light afterward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the floor joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plenty of basement water arrives where the wall meets the slab, not through the wall face. Coat the bottom few inches of the wall carefully and seal the cove joint with a product made for it. If water still comes up along that joint, a coating will not stop it; an interior drain will.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over old paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most masonry waterproofers and every penetrating sealer need bare masonry to work as labeled. Brushing a new coat over sound latex paint gives you a waterproofer bonded to paint that is bonded to the wall, which is only as strong as that old bond. Strip or grind it off first, or choose a different approach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing before fixing gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cheapest waterproofing on any house is moving roof water away from the foundation. Coating the inside of a wall while downspouts dump next to it is working backward. Extend downspouts several feet, regrade low spots, then coat the wall if it is still damp after a few heavy rains.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer works best on a basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a bare block or concrete wall that seeps or stays damp, a cement-and-latex masonry waterproofer such as DRYLOK Extreme is the most dependable choice, because it carries a published hydrostatic pressure rating and fills pores. For unpainted poured concrete where you want no visible film, a penetrating sealer like RadonSeal Plus is the better pick. Neither replaces drainage when water actually runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an interior sealer hold back outside water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and most homeowners do. Interior sealers work against water that has already reached the wall, so they suit dampness and slow seepage best. They are less reliable against heavy, sustained pressure, because the water is pushing the coating away from the masonry. Fixing gutters and grading outside first makes any interior sealer much more likely to last.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one coat of sealer enough on a basement wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Masonry waterproofers call for two coats on most walls, with the first worked in by brush. Rough or split-face block may need a third pass on stubborn pinholes. Penetrating sealers usually take one saturating coat, sometimes a second wet-on-wet application on very porous block. Always follow the specific label, since recoat windows vary by product and temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why pick DRYLOK over Flex Seal for a whole wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an interior basement wall, DRYLOK is the better tool. It is formulated to bond into masonry pores and carries a pressure rating, while Flex Seal Liquid is a general-purpose rubber coating better suited to small patches, flashing and exterior spots. Flex Seal can work on a damp-proofing touch-up, but it is a poor value for coating an entire foundation wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a basement wall sealer stop mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can help indirectly. A sealer that reduces moisture wicking through the wall takes away one source of the dampness mold needs, and some masonry waterproofers include a mildewcide in the film. It will not stop mold caused by humid air condensing on surfaces. Keep relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally 30 to 50 percent, to control mold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does basement waterproofing paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-prepared, properly applied masonry waterproofer typically lasts five to ten years on an interior wall before it needs touch-ups, and sometimes much longer. The biggest factor is how much water reaches the wall. Coatings fail early when efflorescence was left underneath, when the first coat was rolled thin, or when gutters and grading still send water against the foundation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over a basement wall sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over a masonry waterproofer, usually yes, with a latex paint once the coating has cured, though many owners skip it because waterproofers come tinted. Over a penetrating sealer, often no: silane-siloxane repellents in particular can prevent paint from bonding. If you plan to paint, choose your sealer with that in mind and check the data sheet for compatibility.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

